Identify the fastest-growing regions
Determine dominant industries within each region
Evaluate economic volatility across sectors
Statistical Analysis
Analysis of Variance (ANOVA)

Tests whether GDP growth differs significantly across regions and industries.

Null Hypothesis
H0:μ1=μ2=⋯=μk
	​
Alternative Hypothesis
At least one group mean differs.

Assumption Testing

Prior to ANOVA:

Shapiro-Wilk Test (Normality)
Levene's Test (Equal Variance)

When assumptions are violated:

Kruskal-Wallis Test
Multiple Hypothesis Testing

Pairwise regional comparisons are performed using:

Welch's t-tests
Bonferroni Correction
Benjamini-Hochberg False Discovery Rate (FDR)

This identifies specific regions with statistically significant differences in growth.

Effect Size Analysis

Beyond statistical significance:

Cohen's d
Eta Squared (η²)

Effect sizes quantify the practical importance of observed differences.

Correlation Analysis

Correlation matrices identify industries and regions with similar economic behavior.

Analyses
Regional GDP Correlations
Industry GDP Correlations
Visualizations
Correlation Heatmaps
Clustered Correlation Maps
Principal Component Analysis (PCA)

PCA reduces dimensionality and identifies the primary drivers of economic variation.

Objectives
Identify dominant economic structures
Compare regional economic profiles
Visualize similarities among regions
Outputs
Explained Variance Analysis
PCA Loadings
PC1 vs PC2 Scatterplots
